Hmm wait — careful. If `_headerFacts` returns null for unsafe cwd, the session is "unidentified" → skipped → NOT deleted at all (transcript survives). Is that the desired behavior for the RED test? The reviewer: "Require absolute, ..-free cwds before measuring/deleting spills; skip with a note otherwise." — "skip" the spill handling. But if I gate the whole header on cwd validity, the whole session becomes unsweepable — stronger than requested but fail-closed and matches "consider validating in _headerFacts at parse time so one guard covers both uses". A session with a corrupt cwd header is suspect; not sweeping it at all is the safe choice. I'll do that, and the note explains it. Test asserts: (1) the outside spill file survives, (2) the transcript survives (fail-closed), (3) a note mentions the unsafe cwd.

